# voice-mulaw-wrap.hex — a mini-frame stream crossing the 16-bit time-stamp
# wrap. Hand-built octet by octet from RFC 5456 (provenance rule 1,
# Tests/FIXTURES.md).
#
# Direction: peer (source call number 0x0042) → us (0x0001).
#
# The point of this fixture is the boundary in §8.1.2:
#
#   "Mini frames carry a 16-bit time-stamp, which is the lower 16 bits of the
#    transmitting peer's full 32-bit time-stamp for the call… The 16-bit
#    time-stamp wraps after 65.536 seconds."
#
# Datagram 1 is a full Voice frame at 32-bit time-stamp 0x0000FFB0 (65456 ms).
# It pins the codec (µ-law, subclass 0x82) and gives the receiver an
# authoritative 32-bit anchor (§8.1.1, §8.1.2, §6.10).
#
# Datagrams 2–7 are Mini Frames stepping 20 ms each. Their 16-bit fields are
#
#   ffc4  ffd8  ffec  0000  0014  0028
#
# — the first three in the 0x0000xxxx epoch, the last three in 0x0001xxxx.
# Correctly expanded against the peer's 32-bit clock they read 65476, 65496,
# 65516, 65536, 65556, 65576: a plain 20 ms grid with no discontinuity
# anywhere. Expanded by naively carrying the *current* high half forward, a
# frame at 0xffec that arrives after the clock has crossed the boundary
# becomes 131052 — 65536 ms into the future.
#
# Payloads are 160 octets each; every octet of frame k is 0xFF − k, so the
# seven frames decode to the constant levels 0, 8, 16, 24, 32, 40, 48.
#
8042 0001 0000ffb0 02 01 02 82 
0042 ffc4 
0042 ffd8 
0042 ffec 
0042 0000 
0042 0014 
0042 0028 
